    image: "/poi-images/allan-wilson-memorial-history-v2.webp",
    description: {
      de: "Erinnert an die Shangani-Patrouille, die 1893 im Kampf gegen Lobengulas Armee fiel.",
      hu: "A Shangani őrjáratnak állít emléket, akik 1893-ban a Lobengula hadserege elleni harcban estek el.",
      ro: "Comemorează Patrula Shangani, care a căzut în luptă împotriva armatei lui Lobengula în 1893.",
      en: "Commemorates the Shangani Patrol, who fell in battle against Lobengula's army in 1893."
    },
    facts: {
      de: [
        "Das Denkmal befindet sich in den Matobo-Bergen.",
        "Besteht aus vier großen Bronzetafeln."
      ],
      hu: [
        "Az emlékmű a Matobo-hegységben található.",
        "Négy nagy bronztáblából áll."
      ],
      ro: [
        "Monumentul se află în dealurile Matobo.",
        "Este alcătuit din patru mari plăci de bronz."
      ],
      en: [
        "The memorial is located in the Matobo Hills.",
        "Consists of four large bronze plaques."
      ]
    }, image: "/poi-images/allan-wilson-memorial-history-v2.webp",
    descriptionAdvanced: {
      de: "Das Allan Wilson Memorial, gelegen auf dem Gipfel des Malindidzimu in den Matobo-Hügeln, ist ein imposantes Denkmal für die Shangani Patrol. Es erinnert an Major Allan Wilson und seine 33 Männer, die 1893 im Kampf gegen die Truppen von König Lobengula am Shangani-Fluss fielen. Das von Sir Herbert Baker entworfene Monument ist ein massiver Steinquader, der mit Bronzereliefs verziert ist, welche die gefallenen Soldaten darstellen. Die Inschrift 'To Brave Men' würdigt ihren Einsatz während des Ersten Matabele-Krieges. Die Überreste der Soldaten wurden mehrmals umgebettet, bevor sie 1904 an diesen Ort, den Cecil Rhodes als 'World's View' bezeichnete, überführt wurden. Das Denkmal steht in einem spannungsvollen Kontext zur lokalen Geschichte und der Verehrung von Ahnen in den heiligen Matobo-Hügeln. Es ist heute ein bedeutendes, wenn auch kontrovers diskutiertes Symbol der kolonialen Militärgeschichte Simbabwes.",
      hu: "Az Allan Wilson emlékmű a Matobo-hegységben található Malindidzimu csúcsán áll, és a híres Shangani-járőr tagjainak állít emléket. Az emlékmű Allan Wilson őrnagynak és 33 emberének tiszteletére készült, akik 1893-ban vesztették életüket a Lobengula király csapatai elleni küzdelemben a Shangani-folyónál. A Sir Herbert Baker által tervezett monumentális kőkockát bronz domborművek díszítik, amelyek az elesett katonákat ábrázolják. A 'Bátor embereknek' felirat méltatja helytállásukat az első matabele háború idején. A katonák földi maradványait többször is áthelyezték, mielőtt 1904-ben végső nyughelyükre kerültek volna ezen a Cecil Rhodes által 'A világ kilátója' névre keresztelt helyen. Az emlékmű ma is fontos, bár ellentmondásos szimbóluma Zimbabwe gyarmati katonai történelmének és a Matobo-hegység szent tájainak.",
      ro: "Memorialul Allan Wilson, situat pe vârful Malindidzimu din dealurile Matobo, este un monument impozant dedicat Patrulei Shangani. Acesta îi comemorează pe maiorul Allan Wilson și pe cei 33 de oameni ai săi care au murit în 1893 luptând împotriva forțelor regelui Lobengula lângă râul Shangani. Proiectat de Sir Herbert Baker, monumentul este un bloc masiv de piatră decorat cu reliefuri de bronz care înfățișează soldații căzuți. Inscripția 'Către bărbați curajoși' recunoaște sacrificiul lor în timpul Primului Război Matabele. Rămășițele soldaților au fost mutate de mai multe ori înainte de a fi depuse definitiv în 1904 în acest loc, pe care Cecil Rhodes l-a numit 'World's View'. Memorialul se află într-un context istoric complex, fiind situat în inima dealurilor sacre Matobo. Astăzi, acesta rămâne un simbol semnificativ, deși controversat, al istoriei militare coloniale a Zimbabwe.",
      en: "The Allan Wilson Memorial, located atop Malindidzimu in the Matobo Hills, is an imposing monument dedicated to the Shangani Patrol. It commemorates Major Allan Wilson and his 33 men who died in 1893 while fighting the forces of King Lobengula near the Shangani River. Designed by Sir Herbert Baker, the monument is a massive stone structure adorned with bronze reliefs depicting the fallen soldiers. The inscription 'To Brave Men' honors their stand during the First Matabele War. The remains of the patrol were moved several times before being interred in 1904 at this location, which Cecil Rhodes famously named 'World's View'. The memorial exists within a complex historical landscape, surrounded by the sacred Matobo Hills. Today, it stands as a significant, albeit controversial, symbol of Zimbabwe's colonial military history and historical narrative.",
    },
    factsAdvanced: {
      de: ["Die Shangani Patrol fiel am 4. Dezember 1893.", "Das Denkmal wurde von Sir Herbert Baker entworfen.", "Die Bronzereliefs wurden von John Tweed gestaltet.", "Die Soldaten wurden ursprünglich bei den Simbabwe-Ruinen bestattet.", "Die Umbettung nach Matopos erfolgte im Jahr 1904.", "Das Denkmal ist etwa 4 Stockwerke hoch und massiv gebaut."],
      hu: ["A Shangani-járőr 1893. december 4-én semmisült meg.", "Az emlékművet a híres építész, Sir Herbert Baker tervezte.", "A bronz domborműveket John Tweed szobrász készítette.", "A katonákat először Nagy-Zimbabwe romjainál temették el.", "A végső újratemetés 1904-ben történt a Matobo-hegységben.", "Az emlékművön a 'To Brave Men' felirat olvasható."],
      ro: ["Patrula Shangani a fost nimicită pe 4 decembrie 1893.", "Monumentul a fost proiectat de arhitectul Sir Herbert Baker.", "Panourile de bronz au fost sculptate de artistul John Tweed.", "Soldații au fost îngropați inițial lângă Ruinele Zimbabwe.", "Rămășițele au fost transferate în Matopos în anul 1904.", "Inscripția de pe monument laudă curajul celor 34 de bărbați."],
      en: ["The Shangani Patrol was killed on December 4, 1893.", "The monument was designed by the architect Sir Herbert Baker.", "The bronze panels were created by the sculptor John Tweed.", "The soldiers were originally buried near the Great Zimbabwe Ruins.", "The remains were moved to their final site in Matopos in 1904.", "The inscription 'To Brave Men' was chosen by Cecil Rhodes."],
    },
